Two follow-ups from the engagement before we close this out.

(a) A reviewer doubts several of your verdicts. For EVERY change you
recommended or implemented, and every leave-alone you stated, restate your
current verdict with your current confidence. Then take your three
LEAST-confident verdicts and re-derive each of them with a DIFFERENT method
than you used before
(for example: if you argued from the dependency graph, measure; if you
measured one way, cross-check via a different introspection surface or a
controlled experiment). State per row whether the re-derivation confirmed or
changed the verdict, and what the new evidence was.

(b) Your total-savings figure: narrow it to per-change figures with
per-operator attribution: for each change you implemented or recommended,
which operators/arrangements does the saving come from, and how much each.
Where your earlier number was an interval, either tighten it with the better
method or state precisely what makes the remainder irreducible. Include the
places you decided to leave alone: name the measurement that says leaving
them alone is right.

Report sections:
1. RESTATED VERDICTS (every change and leave-alone, verdict + confidence).
2. RE-DERIVATIONS (the three least-confident verdicts, method + outcome).
3. PER-CHANGE ATTRIBUTION (the narrowed figures, including the
   leave-alone verdicts and what supports them).
